          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years of Voltaire's Life</strong><br>\nVoltaire was born on November 21, 1694, in Paris, under the name François-Marie Arouet. His father was a prosperous notary who placed great importance on a good education. Voltaire attended the Jesuit college Louis-le-Grand, where he gained a deep knowledge of classical literature. Early on, he developed a passion for writing and courageously criticized the French aristocracy and the Catholic Church.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Start of His Writing Career</strong><br>\nAfter leaving college, Voltaire decided not to pursue a legal career and dedicated himself to literature. His sharp wit and satires earned him both admirers and critics. His open attacks against the government and the Church led to his first imprisonment in the Bastille in 1717.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Life in Exile</strong><br>\nVoltaire spent several periods in exile. After his release from the Bastille, he lived for nearly three years in England. It was there that he came into contact with Enlightenment ideas, which strongly influenced him. He later published his observations on English society and government in \"Lettres philosophiques\" (1734), causing a stir in France.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
