          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophical Opinions</strong><br>\nVoltaire was a fervent advocate of tolerance, reason, and individual freedom. He criticized the optimism of his time and argued that all was not for the best in the best of all possible worlds, as his contemporary Leibniz claimed. His most famous work, Candide or Optimism, reflects this viewpoint.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Struggle Against Injustice</strong><br>\nOne of Voltaire's main concerns was the fight against injustice. He used his writings to denounce abuses and called for reforms to the French judicial system. His commitment to overturning the Calas affair, in which an innocent man was executed, is a famous example of his activism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Works</strong><br>\nVoltaire was a prolific writer who left behind a varied body of work, including plays, poems, novels, and essays. He wrote more than 20,000 letters and over 2,000 books and pamphlets. He was known for his sharp and often biting humor, which made him unpopular among the powerful.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "fr"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
