            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with Power</strong><br>\nAlthough Voltaire often clashed with authority, he maintained relationships with some influential figures of his time, including Frederick II of Prussia. He spent time at the court of the latter, where he served as an advisor and court poet, but again faced conflicts due to his outspoken opinions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Last Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Voltaire returned to France and was celebrated as a literary celebrity. He died in Paris on May 30, 1778. Today, he is considered one of the greatest thinkers of the Enlightenment, and his writings have had a lasting influence on European culture and philosophy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Voltaire and the Enlightenment</strong><br>\nVoltaire was a symbol of the Enlightenment, a movement that represented reason, science, and the progress of human society. His work had a decisive influence on the development of modern political and philosophical thought. His legacy endures in the appreciation of freedom of expression and critical thinking.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
